"""In-place ``aten::_foreach_log10_`` implemented with Triton kernels.

Semantics: for every tensor ``T`` in the list, every logical element is replaced
by ``log10(x)``; storage, stride, dtype and shape are unchanged and the call
returns ``None``.  Integral / bool inputs are rejected (an in-place op cannot
narrow a promoted float result back into an integer dtype), matching ATen.

Three structural choices drive the latency:

* Each program walks ``NTILE`` contiguous tiles in a pipelined loop, and the
  load pointer and the store pointer are passed as two distinct kernel
  parameters (the same buffer twice).  Otherwise the alias analysis sees
  ``load(x)`` and ``store(x)`` through one value and refuses to overlap them;
  the reordering it enables only crosses disjoint tiles of one tensor, so the
  in-place result is unchanged.  This lifted the 1G-element workloads from
  ~762 GB/s to ~1.11 TB/s.
* Launch count is minimised for small tensors, because one triton launch costs
  far more than their whole kernel.  A small homogeneous device/dtype/
  contiguous group is served by a *single* launch with grid
  ``(n_tensors, chunks)``; its per-program pointer arrives as an int64 kernel
  argument (no host->device metadata copy, no allocation).
* That fused form is deliberately *not* used for large tensors: selecting the
  pointer in-kernel costs a dependent scalar prologue and gives up the pointer
  alignment information of a real pointer argument, which measured ~694 GB/s
  against the ~1.11 TB/s of the plain form.  A tensor whose own device time
  (~35us) can hide a triton launch is therefore dispatched as its own launch.
"""

